{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773766171,"nonce":"urQnmv8mhxtj4wXoA04P9DrYjnc=","title":"launch.py should remove stale container before starting a new one","message":"When a container dies without being cleaned up (e.g. killed during auth failure retries), the old container stays around with the same name. On resume, docker run fails with 'container name already in use'.\n\n## Current behavior\n\n- Container exits abnormally, docker --rm doesn't clean it up\n- User runs nightshift resume\n- launch.py tries docker run with the same container name\n- Fails: 'Conflict. The container name is already in use'\n- User must manually docker rm -f the old container\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nBefore docker run, launch.py (or docker_cmd.py) should check if a container with the target name already exists and remove it. A simple docker rm -f before run would suffice — it's a no-op if the container doesn't exist.\n\n## Files to modify\n\n- host/docker_cmd.py: run_container should docker rm -f the container name before docker run\n- Tests: verify stale container cleanup before launch\n\nREQ-001","files":null}]}